<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    A global the program only moves through floating-point registers is a
    float (option floatglobals, kuna_floatglobals.rs). The script loads three
    in-tree linked images of floatglobal.c (source beside them): gcc -O2 for
    x86-64, gcc -O2 for AArch64 and for hard-float ARM.

    fy copies its double parameter into gd and ff its float into gf, and no
    other code touches either but through a float register. With the option
    off every global refuses the parameter vote and the parameter prints as an
    integer the convention passes in a general register; on, the whole-program
    scan finds gd and gf float-only and the parameters are floats. set_gpun
    memcpys a float into gpun, which use_gpun adds to as an integer, and clearz
    zeroes the double gz with an integer store: both globals refuse, and
    set_gpun and setz keep their integer parameters. Hard-float ARM drops fy's
    d0 parameter (armfloatargs off), so only ff is asked there.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
